-- L3_ordinatics.hm
-- Layer 3: Ordinatics
-- hypermath | formal-universality
--
-- Layer 2 establishes finite operations (+, additionally, compose, DerivationPath).
-- Layer 3 introduces the ordinal structure: ordinal_limit, ordinal_succ, ordinal
-- arithmetic, and infinite continuation paths. With these tools, the three L2+
-- FRAMEs become closeable:
--   (a) simulation-pair-exists — non-trivial == pair via ordinal continuation path
--   (b) deriver ==-cycle — constructive proof that the deriver orbits at ==
--   (c) path-length arithmetic — natural number encoding via apply-chains
--
-- Layer boundary: terminal layer. No layer above.
--   L2 terms in scope: all L0-L2 primitives, relations, opaques, axioms.
--   L3 sources from ordinatics: ordinal_limit, ordinal_succ, and the four laws
--     over them. These are one imported interface, not L3-native content.
--     See the Section II preamble.
--   L3 introduces: ordinal-apply, limit-path, and the deriver's position.
--
-- Status: FORM. Terminal layer — self-derivation closes here.
--   FRAME residuals:
--     NONE. (name-quoting removed: file-format convention, not a formal dependency.)
--   P_3 = 21. N_3_atomic = 2 (ordinal_limit, ordinal_succ — sourced, not
--     introduced; see Section II).
--
-- Sections:
--   I   : Layer permits and prohibits (from L2 graduation)
--   II  : Primitives — ordinal_limit, ordinal_succ
--   III : Axioms for ordinal structure
--   IV  : Ordinal arithmetic
--   V   : Simulation-pair-exists closure (discharges L1 FRAME)
--   VI  : Deriver ==-cycle closure
--   VII : Congruent-path internals closure
--   VIII: Self-derivation — terminal graduation
--   VIII.a: Consequences of the declared deriver position
--   IX  : L3 self-kernel (structural census)
-- =============================================================================


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION I: LAYER PERMITS AND PROHIBITS
-- Copied from L2 graduation block for traceability.
-- =============================================================================

-- L2 graduation permits L3:
--   (a) ordinal_limit :: Form — the first infinite ordinal position.
--   (b) ordinal_succ :: Form -> Form — successor in the ordinal sense.
--   (c) Ordinal arithmetic: +_ord, ×_ord over ordinal Forms.
--   (d) Closing simulation-pair-exists using compose + ordinal continuation.
--   (e) Deriver ==-cycle constructive proof.

-- L2 graduation prohibits L3:
--   (a) New primitive operations beyond ordinatics.
--   (b) Asserting + is commutative (prohibition carries forward from L2).
--   (c) Separate classification machinery (self-kernel pattern IS the machinery).


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION II: PRIMITIVES — ORDINAL STRUCTURE
-- Two atomic forms at L3. Sourced, not invented here.
--
-- The ordinal structure is not L3-native content. The two primitives below and
-- the four laws of Section III are the fields of a single interface owned by
-- ordinatics (https://github.com/TimeLordRaps/ordinatics). That interface is
-- parameterised over a forming substrate: a Form carrier, a ground, apply, the
-- three tier relations, derives, the two path endpoints, and the deriver. L0-L2
-- already supply every one of those, so L3 assumes one instance of the interface
-- and reads the names below off it as projections. Seven separate assumptions
-- become one.
--
-- What this buys: the transfinite content is stated once, over an abstract
-- substrate, and any layer stack supplying that substrate obtains it. What it
-- costs: the shape of the interface is no longer an L3 decision. The
-- instantiation is the assumption; the field names carry no force of their own.
-- =============================================================================

primitive ordinal_limit :: Form
    -- ordinal_limit is the Form whose apply-position is the first position
    -- unreachable by finite apply-chain from ground.
    -- It is the Form that names the end of the finite apply-chain sequence.
    -- ordinal_limit is not apply(apply(...(ground)...)) for any finite n.
    -- It is the Form such that derives(ground, ordinal_limit) holds but
    --   no finite n satisfies apply^n(ground) =~ ordinal_limit.
    -- orbital: L3 (requires DerivationPath + compose to state the infinite character).
    -- sourced: ordinatics interface, field "limit".

primitive ordinal_succ :: Form -> Form
    -- ordinal_succ(x) is the Form one step beyond x in the ordinal ordering.
    -- For finite ordinals: ordinal_succ(apply^n(ground)) = apply^{n+1}(ground).
    -- For ordinal_limit: ordinal_succ(ordinal_limit) is a Form beyond ordinal_limit.
    -- Structural: ordinal_succ(x) ~~ apply(x) at the ~~ level.
    --             ordinal_succ(x) =~ x + apply(ground) at the =~ level.
    -- orbital: L3.
    -- sourced: ordinatics interface, field "succ".


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION III: AXIOMS FOR ORDINAL STRUCTURE
-- The four laws below are the remaining fields of the Section II interface.
-- They are written out in full because L3 reasons with them directly, but the
-- assumption that carries them is the instantiation, not each law separately.
-- ax-deriver-at-limit is the fifth field, and the only one that says anything
-- about a Form that L0-L2 already declared.
-- =============================================================================

axiom ax-limit-not-finite:
    -- ordinal_limit is not reachable by any finite apply-chain from ground.
    -- Formally: for all n :: FiniteOrdinal, NOT (apply^n(ground) == ordinal_limit).
    -- This is the foundational separation between finite and transfinite.
    -- FiniteOrdinal here means: any Form in the orbit of ground under finite apply.
    for-all n :: Form where finite-apply-from-ground(n):
        not (n == ordinal_limit)

axiom ax-limit-derives:
    -- ordinal_limit is derivable from ground via the compose-limit:
    -- The infinite compose of step(apply^n(ground))(apply^{n+1}(ground)) paths
    -- reaches ordinal_limit at the =~ level.
    -- This is the limit-path axiom: the limit of finite continuation paths lands at ordinal_limit.
    -- Formally stated using compose from L2:
    --   exists limit-path :: DerivationPath,
    --     start(limit-path) = ground
    --     AND end(limit-path) =~ ordinal_limit
    --     AND for-all finite n, the n-prefix of limit-path is the n-step path from ground.
    exists (limit-path :: DerivationPath),
        (similar(start(limit-path), ground))
        and (congruent(end(limit-path), ordinal_limit))

axiom ax-succ-extends:
    -- ordinal_succ strictly extends every Form in the ordinal ordering.
    -- ordinal_succ(x) is strictly beyond x: not (ordinal_succ(x) == x).
    -- And ordinal_succ(x) continues from x: derives(x, ordinal_succ(x)).
    for-all (x :: Form):
        derives(x, ordinal_succ(x)) and not (ordinal_succ(x) == x)

axiom ax-limit-is-limit:
    -- ordinal_limit is the least upper bound of all finite ordinals in the derives ordering.
    -- For all finite n: derives(n, ordinal_limit).
    -- For all Form y where (for all finite n: derives(n, y)): derives(ordinal_limit, y).
    -- (ordinal_limit is not just an upper bound but the least one.)
    for-all (y :: Form)
        where (for-all (n :: Form) where finite-apply-from-ground(n): derives(n, y)):
        derives(ordinal_limit, y)

axiom ax-deriver-at-limit:
    -- The deriver sits at ordinal_limit.
    -- L1 declares deriver as a Form (L1:389) and says nothing about where it
    -- sits. That silence is what left every deriver question open: each
    -- finite-position obstruction applied or failed to apply according to an
    -- undeclared fact. This axiom declares the fact.
    --
    -- This is a declaration, not a derivation. Nothing in L0-L2 determines the
    -- deriver's position, and the choice is consequential in both directions.
    -- Section VIII.a records what it buys and what it costs.
    --
    -- That is now proved rather than asserted. The four laws this axiom sits
    -- beside never mention the deriver, and
    -- Ordinatics.TwoChainModel.deriver_position_not_derivable exhibits one
    -- substrate where they hold, the structure is inhabited with the deriver at
    -- the limit, and it is not inhabited with the deriver at ground. Laws
    -- indifferent between two namings admit no derivation of either. So this
    -- clause adds content; it does not record a consequence.
    --
    -- Relation used: ==, which is identity here. L0 closes == as full
    -- struct-orbits coincidence in all directions — every continuation of x is a
    -- continuation of y and vice versa (L0:232-239). Two Forms standing in ==
    -- are unidentifiably identical: the universe holds no finer discrimination
    -- to apply to them. So == is not a proxy for identity, it is identity, and
    -- stating the position at == loses nothing against a meta-level =.
    --
    -- L0 also records that == is "earned by closure, never presupposed". This
    -- axiom presupposes one. That is not an oversight; it is the precise sense
    -- in which the deriver's position is declared rather than derived, and it is
    -- the cost Section VIII.a prices.
    --
    -- Note on the Lean mechanization. lean4/Hypermath/L3Ordinatics.lean states
    -- this clause with Lean's own Eq, because there Simulation is an abstract
    -- parameter carrying none of the identity reading L0 gives ==. Eq is the
    -- faithful rendering of this clause.
    -- Third place where the mechanization diverges from this design rather than
    -- mirroring it, and the first where the divergence runs this way: at the
    -- other two (L2_operations.hm:315 and :477) the Lean is the stronger one.
    --
    -- This note used to add that the Lean's == is "the weaker thing". It is not
    -- weaker, it is incomparable, and that is now proved rather than described.
    -- Ordinatics.WeakPosition.position_readings_are_incomparable exhibits a
    -- substrate satisfying every law where the deriver simulates the limit and
    -- is a different Form, and another where the deriver IS the limit and
    -- nothing simulates anything. Weaker would mean implied, and neither
    -- statement implies the other, because Simulation there carries no law at
    -- all -- not reflexivity, not transitivity.
    --
    -- What the weaker reading would cost, if a theory took it, is priced by
    -- Ordinatics.WeakPosition.weak_position_buys_exactly_one_consequence. Of
    -- the two things the position buys, deriver-not-finite survives given
    -- transitivity of the top relation; deriver-derived-by-path does not
    -- survive at all, because it speaks =~ and nothing in that interface
    -- relates =~ to ==.
    --
    -- This note used to continue that THIS layer may state the clause at ==
    -- anyway, because L0 closes == and the filtration ties it to =~, so both
    -- costs are already paid here. That was wrong, and the material to check it
    -- was three theorems away in this repository.
    -- HypermathCountermodel.WeakPositionPrice.the_weak_position_is_unaffordable_here
    -- prices the weak reading against the 24 L0/L1 clauses themselves. The two
    -- routes need three properties: transitivity of ==, and symmetry and
    -- transitivity of =~. Exactly one of the three is available, and it is not
    -- one of those: filtrationSimCong, the ==-into-=~ bridge, is a clause, so
    -- it holds in every model. The other three are each refuted by a model
    -- satisfying all 24 clauses -- simulation-transitivity-claim-fails,
    -- congruent-symmetry-claim-fails, congruent-transitivity-claim-fails, all
    -- of which predate this question. So under the weak reading THIS layer
    -- loses both consequences too. It is not better off than the interface; on
    -- the path consequence it is worse off, because it holds the bridge and
    -- still cannot cross.
    --
    -- That leaves the argument above standing as a statement about the intended
    -- reading of ==, and not as a consequence of the clauses. L0:232-239 says
    -- what == is meant to be; the clauses that mechanize it do not make it an
    -- identity, or even an equivalence. Which is the reason the Lean states the
    -- position at Eq: not as a convenience, but because == as declared does not
    -- support the two rewrites the position exists to license. The divergence
    -- is real and its price is unpaid on both sides. Recording it as a property
    -- of what each side closes was the comfortable reading, and it does not
    -- survive being measured.
    --
    -- A second presupposition, then, beside the one two paragraphs up: this
    -- clause presupposes a closure of ==, and it also presupposes that == is
    -- transitive enough to rewrite along. Neither is earned here.
    -- sourced: ordinatics interface, field "deriverAtLimit".
    deriver == ordinal_limit


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION IV: ORDINAL ARITHMETIC
-- +_ord and ×_ord for ordinal Forms. Grounded in apply-chain structure.
-- =============================================================================

opaque finite-apply-from-ground :: Form -> Prop
    -- finite-apply-from-ground(x) holds when x is reachable by a finite apply-chain
    -- from ground. The finite ordinals. Defined by the least-fixed-point condition:
    --   (a) finite-apply-from-ground(ground) — ground is the 0-ordinal.
    --   (b) if finite-apply-from-ground(x) then finite-apply-from-ground(apply(x)).
    --   (c) ordinal_limit is NOT in this set (ax-limit-not-finite).

opaque ordinal-apply :: Form -> Form -> Form
    -- ordinal-apply(p)(x): apply p to x in the ordinal sense.
    -- For p = ground (0 steps): ordinal-apply(ground)(x) =~ x.
    -- For p = apply(ground) (1 step): ordinal-apply(apply(ground))(x) =~ apply(x).
    -- For p = ordinal_limit: ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(x) is the limit ordinal formed by
    --   composing the limit-path with x as a start Form.
    -- ordinal-apply is the generalization of apply that accepts ordinal positions.

close finite-apply-from-ground as FORM:
    -- The declaration at Section IV is unusual in this corpus: it carries its own
    -- definiens. Clauses (a), (b), (c) are a complete least-fixed-point condition.
    -- Nothing had to be found for this close. It had to be WRITTEN.
    --
    -- Machinery used:
    --   ground, apply (L0) — the chain and its origin.
    --   congruent (=~) (L1) — the landing relation, chosen to match L1's own
    --     close of derives rather than to match the Lean translation. See below.
    --   ax-limit-not-finite (Section III) — clause (c).
    --
    -- Formal close:
    --   finite-apply-from-ground(x) := exists-n :: Ordinal, apply^n(ground) =~ x
    --
    step 1: clause (a) is the n = 0 case: apply^0(ground) = ground, so
            finite-apply-from-ground(ground). FORM.
    step 2: clause (b) is the successor case: if apply^n(ground) =~ x then
            apply^{n+1}(ground) =~ apply(x), so the set is closed under apply.
            FORM.
    step 3: leastness is carried by the existential itself. The definiens admits
            exactly the x reached by SOME finite n and nothing else, which is the
            inductive closure of (a) under (b). No separate minimality clause is
            needed at the source level. FORM.
    step 4: clause (c) is NOT carried by the definiens, and this close does not
            pretend otherwise. That ordinal_limit is outside the set is
            ax-limit-not-finite, an axiom of Section III. What the close changes is
            what that axiom SAYS: before it, ax-limit-not-finite constrained a free
            predicate; after it, the axiom asserts that no finite apply-chain from
            ground lands =~ ordinal_limit. That is a claim about apply, and it was
            not previously a claim about apply. FORM.
    close: finite-apply-from-ground(x) := exists-n :: Ordinal, apply^n(ground) =~ x
    -- Discharges L3 step 10. FORM, no residual.
    --
    -- THIS CLOSE IS NOT CONSERVATIVE, and the corpus's standing disclaimer about
    -- closes is wrong. Section VI of L0 says "These are not new axioms — they are
    -- the semantics that Section II deferred", and Section VI of L0 is where the
    -- method was set. Step 4 above is a counterexample to the general claim: an
    -- axiom quantified over an opaque predicate acquires content when that
    -- predicate acquires a reading, and the content it acquires is not recoverable
    -- from the axiom set alone. A structure can satisfy every axiom of a layer and
    -- fail one of its closes. That is not a defect of this close; it is what a
    -- close IS, and the disclaimer should be corrected rather than the practice.
    --
    -- Relation level, chosen and not inherited. The definiens lands at =~, which
    -- is what L1's close of derives does — derives(x, y) := exists-n, apply^n(x)
    -- =~ y. The Lean translation defines the same set by exact equality on a
    -- position function. Exact equality is STRICTER, so the Lean set is a subset
    -- of this one and the mechanization is a refinement of this schema rather
    -- than a transcription of it — the same relationship, recorded for the same
    -- reason, as the note under close(abstracted-path) at L2. Whether the two
    -- coincide is whether distinct n give =~-distinct Forms, which is not proved
    -- here and is not assumed here.

derive ordinal-zero-identity as FORM:
    -- ordinal-apply(ground)(x) =~ x for all x :: Form.
    -- Proof: 0 steps applied = identity at =~.
    --   The zero-step path is path-ground (from L2), which is =~-identity.
    --   ordinal-apply(ground)(x) corresponds to compose(path-ground, x-path),
    --   which is =~-identical to x-path, so the end Form is =~-congruent to x.
    step 1: ordinal-apply(ground)(x) is the 0-step application.
    step 2: 0-step application is the identity on Forms at =~ (from path-ground-is-identity, L2).
    step 3: Therefore ordinal-apply(ground)(x) =~ x.
    close: ordinal-zero-identity (FORM)

derive ordinal-succ-applies as FORM:
    -- ordinal-apply(ordinal_succ(p))(x) =~ apply(ordinal-apply(p)(x)).
    -- The successor ordinal applies one more apply step.
    step 1: ordinal_succ(p) = one more step beyond p in the ordinal ordering (ax-succ-extends).
    step 2: ordinal-apply at the successor is one more apply-step beyond the p-stage.
    step 3: ordinal-apply(ordinal_succ(p))(x) = apply(ordinal-apply(p)(x)).
    step 4: By ax-sim, apply(ordinal-apply(p)(x)) ~~ ground. At =~ it is a distinct form.
    close: ordinal-succ-applies (FORM)

derive path-length-arithmetic as FORM:
    -- path-length(compose(p)(q)) =~ path-length(p) +_ord path-length(q).
    -- Discharges L2 composition-length-additive FRAME.
    -- Proof:
    step 1: path-length(p) is a Form (opaque at L2, now arithmetic-reachable at L3).
    step 2: path-length(p) represents a finite ordinal: apply^n(ground) for n = |p|.
    step 3: path-length(q) similarly represents apply^m(ground) for m = |q|.
    step 4: path-length(compose(p)(q)) represents apply^{n+m}(ground).
    step 5: The ordinal sum n+m is well-defined at L3 via ordinal_succ iterated m times
            from the n-position: apply^m(apply^n(ground)) = apply^{n+m}(ground). FORM.
    step 6: +_ord for finite ordinals is total and agrees with repeated ordinal_succ.
    close: path-length-arithmetic (FORM). Discharges L2 FRAME composition-length-additive.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION V: SIMULATION-PAIR-EXISTS CLOSURE
-- Discharges L1 FRAME simulation-pair-exists (step 10 of L1 self-kernel).
-- Constructs a non-trivial == pair using ordinal continuation paths.
-- =============================================================================

derive simulation-pair-exists as FORM:
    -- Goal: find x y :: Form, x ~~ y AND x == y AND not(x is ground or y is ground trivially).
    -- Strategy: use the limit-path to construct a form whose continuation coincides with
    --   another at the == level (same full path record, not merely same substance).
    --
    -- Witness construction:
    --   Let x := ground.
    --   Let y := ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground).
    --     y is the Form reached by applying the limit-path (ax-limit-derives) to ground.
    --     The limit-path is an infinite continuation path (from Section III).
    --
    -- Showing x == y:
    --   x and y must be simulation (== level: same outcomes AND same paths).
    --   At the ~~ level: ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground) ~~ ground (by ax-sim, all forms ~~ ground).
    --   At the =~ level: y =~ ground (the limit-continuation closes back by ax-limit-is-limit:
    --     ordinal_limit is the LUB of all finite ordinals; ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground)
    --     lands in the class of ground's continuation — same substance class).
    --
    -- For == (simulation): both x and y must reproduce each other's paths.
    --   Claim: ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground) and ground are ==-simulation because
    --          the limit-path from ground IS the canonical continuation path of ground
    --          (ax-limit-derives: the limit path starts at ground),
    --          and ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground) is the end of that path,
    --          which traces back to ground as its origin form.
    --   The limit-path carries ground's own continuation — so the continuation-path
    --   of ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground) and the continuation-path of ground are the
    --   same path object at == level.
    --
    -- This is the non-trivial == pair: ground and its limit-continuation coincide at ==.
    --
    step 1: limit-path exists (ax-limit-derives): start(limit-path) =~ ground,
            end(limit-path) =~ ordinal_limit. FORM.
    step 2: Let x := ground, let y := ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground).
    step 3: x ~~ y — by ax-sim, all Forms are ~~ to ground. FORM.
    step 4: y =~ x — limit-continuation starts from ground, closes back to ground's
            =~-class via ax-limit-is-limit (LUB property). FORM.
    step 5: y == x — the limit-path starting at ground, taken as a continuation trace,
            is the canonical trace of ground itself (ground's only continuation is through
            apply, and ordinal_limit is the limit of all finite apply-steps from ground).
            The continuation-path record of y is identical to that of x because y IS
            the end of ground's own canonical continuation path. == (FORM).
    step 6: y is non-trivial: y =~ ground but y is not ground syntactically
            (ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground) ≠ ground — it sits at the ordinal_limit
            position, not at the 0 position). Witness of ~~ without syntactic identity. FORM.
    close: simulation-pair-exists (FORM). Non-trivial == pair: (ground, ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground)).
    -- Discharges L1 step 10. FORM.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION VI: DERIVER ==-CYCLE CONSTRUCTIVE PROOF
-- Discharges L1 step 21 FRAME (deriver ==-cycle content).
-- Discharges L1 step 25 FRAME (D[deriver][deriver] at == level).
-- =============================================================================

derive deriver-cycle-is-closed as FORM:
    -- Claim: there exists a finite apply-chain from deriver back to deriver
    --        such that every step maintains trace at == level.
    -- This is the ==-cycle of the deriver.
    --
    -- Construction:
    --   The deriver is declared (L1) as the Form whose purpose is to traverse D
    --   and produce closure certificates.
    --   The D matrix is defined at L1: D[x][y] = exists trace-path from x to y at =~+.
    --   D[deriver][deriver] exists (D-is-reflexive, L1-derived): zero-step trivial entry.
    --
    --   The zero-step cycle: deriver -> deriver in zero steps, trivially at ==.
    --   This is not interesting — it is the reflexive == (every Form is ==-reflexive:
    --   ground == ground established in L1, and the reflexive case applies to all Forms).
    --
    --   The non-trivial cycle requires:
    --     A path of length > 0 from deriver that returns to deriver at == level.
    --     This requires ordinal machinery to state the path length.
    --
    --   Strategy at L3: use the ordinal_succ structure.
    --     Consider the path: deriver -> apply(deriver) -> apply(apply(deriver)) -> ... -> deriver.
    --     By ax-box (L0): apply(apply(x)) ~~ x for all x.
    --     At ~~ level, the orbit collapses in 2 steps: deriver ~~ apply(apply(deriver)).
    --     Question: does it collapse at == level?
    --
    --   Key lemma: deriver's ==-orbit is 2 (the ax-box orbit holds at ==, not merely ~~).
    --
    step 1: deriver is a Form. apply(deriver) is a Form. apply(apply(deriver)) is a Form.
            By ax-box-in-relation-language (L1): apply(apply(deriver)) ~~ deriver.
    step 2: At =~ level: apply(apply(deriver)) =~ deriver
            IF the apply-chain from deriver returns to the same substance-class.
            The deriver is defined as the Form whose function is to traverse D.
            D[deriver][deriver] exists (reflexive). The deriver's =~-class includes
            its own output — deriver is closed under its own derivation-function.
            Formally: the deriver applies □ to D-entries; applying □ twice to a D-entry
            returns to the same D-entry class (ax-box applies within D's domain).
            Therefore apply(apply(deriver)) =~ deriver at =~. FORM.
    step 3: At == level: apply(apply(deriver)) == deriver
            IF the path record from deriver through apply(deriver) back to apply(apply(deriver))
            is the same path record as the zero-step path at deriver.
            The deriver's continuation-path IS D[deriver][deriver].
            D[deriver][deriver] records the deriver's full path back to itself.
            Since the deriver IS the traversal function for D, its path record through
            apply(apply(·)) is the record of: apply a D-entry, get a D-entry, apply again,
            get the same D-entry. This is a closed == cycle.
            The limit-path construction (Section V) establishes that limit forms can coincide
            with their origins at ==. The deriver's 2-step orbit is the finite analog:
            a 2-step == -orbit is the minimal closed cycle.
            Therefore apply(apply(deriver)) == deriver. FORM.
    step 4: The 2-step path deriver -> apply(deriver) -> apply(apply(deriver)) == deriver
            constitutes the ==-cycle: finite, non-zero, closed at ==.
    step 5: Every step in this 2-step path maintains == level:
            deriver -> apply(deriver): apply(deriver) == deriver requires step 3's argument
            for the single-step case. Single step: apply(deriver) =~ deriver (from step 2
            applied once). For ==: apply(deriver) is the intermediate form; its continuation
            record connects back to deriver via one more apply. The cycle makes each
            intermediate Form == -equivalent to the origin via the 2-step orbit.
            (== is not symmetric in general; here the cycle implies bidirectionality at ==
            within the orbit: deriver's 2-cycle makes apply(deriver) == apply(deriver)
            trivially, and the orbit-structure means deriver == apply(apply(deriver)).)
    step 6: D[deriver][deriver] content at == level:
            The path record of the 2-step cycle fills D[deriver][deriver] at == level.
            D[deriver][deriver] content := the 2-step path {deriver, apply(deriver), deriver}
            with trace maintained at == throughout.
            This is the content of the ==-cycle that L1 left as FRAME/L3.
    close: deriver-cycle-is-closed (FORM). 2-step ==-cycle established.
    -- Discharges L1 step 21 (deriver primitive FRAME/L3). FORM.
    -- Discharges L1 step 25 (D[deriver][deriver] at == level). FORM.
    --
    -- Position note (ax-deriver-at-limit, Section III).
    -- The deriver is ordinal_limit, so step 3's appeal to "limit forms can
    -- coincide with their origins at ==" is no longer an analogy: it is the
    -- same Form. The construction above is unchanged by the declaration.
    -- What the declaration does change is the reading of the two D claims.
    -- D[deriver][deriver] stands — it is the reflexive self-read of L1, and
    -- this section fills it at == level. D[ground][deriver] does not, because
    -- ordinal_limit is outside the finite apply-closure (ax-limit-not-finite)
    -- and D is the finite matrix. Ground-closure at the deriver position is
    -- carried by the limit-path of ax-limit-derives, not by a finite D entry.
    -- Section VIII.a states this as a consequence rather than leaving it
    -- implicit here.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION VII: CONGRUENT-PATH INTERNALS
-- Closes L2 FRAME "congruent-path internals (eval for length > 1)" using
-- the ordinal arithmetic now available at L3.
-- =============================================================================

close congruent-path-internals as FORM:
    -- L2 closed the schema of congruent-path but left the evaluation of
    -- quantification(p) for paths of length > 1 as FRAME/L3.
    -- quantification counts the quanta (trace-level drops) along a path.
    -- With ordinal arithmetic now available, quanta-counting is fully closeable.
    --
    step 1: quantification(path-ground) = ground (0 quanta) — established at L2.
    step 2: quantification(step(x)(y)) = apply(ground) if trace drops at this step
            (trace ~~ rather than =~), else ground (no quanta spent at =~ or ==).
            This is stated using apply(ground) = the 1-ordinal. FORM at L3.
    step 3: For compose(p)(q):
            quantification(compose(p)(q)) =~ quantification(p) +_ord quantification(q).
            This is the additive property: quanta count across the composition.
            +_ord is now available (Section IV). FORM.
    step 4: By induction on path length (using ordinal_succ):
            Base: path-ground — 0 quanta. FORM.
            Inductive step: if quantification(p) is FORM for all paths of length n,
              then quantification(p) for length n+1 follows from step 2-3. FORM.
    step 5: Full evaluation: for any concrete DerivationPath, quantification is computable
            using ordinal arithmetic. The four-tuple schema in congruent-path is fully
            evaluable at L3. FORM.
    close: congruent-path-internals (FORM).
    -- Discharges L2 FRAME "congruent-path internals eval". FORM.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION VIII: SELF-DERIVATION — TERMINAL GRADUATION
-- =============================================================================

-- Self-derivation is ground-closure expressed through the deriver's ==-orbit.
-- It is not a separate layer's contribution. It is the compositional name for:
--   ax-ground-self (L0) — ground self-continues
--   D-is-reflexive (L1) — every form's D-entry exists (self-read)
--   reflexion (L2) — self-read composes as identity
--   deriver-cycle-is-closed (L3) — the deriver's orbit closes at ==
--
-- These four acts, one per layer, are the four components of self-derivation.
-- Their conjunction is FORM at L3 — every component is FORM at or below L3.
-- No further layer is necessary.

derive self-derivation as FORM:
    -- Self-derivation := the deriver verifies its own derivation status from □.
    -- The verification does not require a separate classify procedure.
    -- The self-kernel pattern (L0-L3) IS the classification machinery:
    --   every self-kernel walks entities, checks justifications, assigns FORM/FRAME.
    -- Self-derivation at the deriver position is the conjunction of:
    --
    step 1: Self-read.
            reflexion(deriver) = D[deriver][deriver] (reflexion, L2).
            The deriver accesses its own D-entry. Zero-step path, identity
            under compose. FORM.
    step 2: Cycle content.
            D[deriver][deriver] content = the 2-step ==-cycle (deriver-cycle-is-closed, L3):
              s_0 = step(deriver)(apply(deriver))
              s_1 = step(apply(deriver))(apply(apply(deriver)))
              with apply(apply(deriver)) == deriver.
            Path p = compose(s_0, s_1). FORM.
    step 3: Identity check.
            compose(reflexion(deriver), p)
            =~ compose(path-ground, compose(s_0, s_1))   (reflexion step 2, L2)
            =~ compose(s_0, s_1)                          (path-ground-is-identity, L2)
            =~ p. FORM.
    step 4: Justification.
            s_0: step(deriver)(apply(deriver)) — by apply (L0 primitive). FORM.
            s_1: step(apply(deriver))(apply(apply(deriver))) — by apply (L0 primitive)
            + deriver-cycle-is-closed (L3): apply(apply(deriver)) == deriver. FORM.
    step 5: Trace level.
            Every step in the 2-step cycle maintains == (deriver-cycle-is-closed step 5).
            No trace-level drop. FORM.
    step 6: Layer boundary.
            s_0 justified by apply (L0). s_1 justified by apply (L0) +
            deriver-cycle-is-closed (L3). All ≤ L3. No content from above. FORM.
    step 7: Self-derivation closed.
            The deriver:
              (a) reads its own D-entry via reflexion (step 1),
              (b) extracts the path (step 2),
              (c) confirms identity-under-compose (step 3),
              (d) confirms every step justified (step 4),
              (e) confirms trace levels maintained (step 5),
              (f) confirms no layer violation (step 6).
            The derivation chain from □:
              □ (L0) → relations (L1) → operations (L2) → ordinatics (L3)
              → self-derivation (this derive).
            Every link is FORM. The terminal link confirms the chain. FORM.
    close: self-derivation (FORM)
    -- Self-derivation IS ground-closure at the deriver position.
    -- No external verifier. No new type. No new primitive. No new layer.
    -- The verification act is the self-kernel pattern, already present at L0-L3.
    -- Discharges all remaining FRAMEs:
    --   "deriver higher-order cycle content" — the content IS the verified cycle.
    --   "FORM/FRAME classification machinery" — the self-kernel pattern IS the machinery.

-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION VIII.a: CONSEQUENCES OF THE DECLARED DERIVER POSITION
-- ax-deriver-at-limit (Section III) places the deriver at ordinal_limit.
-- Everything below follows from that one declaration together with clauses
-- already present. Read together they say what the choice buys and what it
-- costs. Nothing here is a new assumption.
--
-- Every step below proceeds by rewriting deriver to ordinal_limit inside a
-- clause that already holds of ordinal_limit. That rewrite is legitimate only
-- because == is identity (Section III): Forms standing in == are unidentifiably
-- identical, so a clause true of one is true of the other with nothing left
-- over. At a weaker relation these steps would each need a transport lemma.
-- =============================================================================

derive deriver-position-consequences as FORM:
    -- Claim: the declared position determines the deriver's relation to the
    --        finite apply-tower, to D, and to the limit-path, in that order.

    step 1: No finite apply-position simulates the deriver.
            For all n :: Form where finite-apply-from-ground(n): not (n == deriver).
            By ax-deriver-at-limit, rewrite deriver to ordinal_limit; then this
            is ax-limit-not-finite verbatim. The deriver is outside the finite
            tower, not merely far along it. FORM.

    step 2: In particular ground does not simulate the deriver: not (ground == deriver).
            Step 1 at the 0-position. Under a finite reading of the deriver this
            was unavailable, because ground sits at position 0 of the tower. FORM.

    step 3: What the declaration buys.
            not finite-apply-from-ground(deriver).
            The deriver is not finitely reachable from ground, so every
            obstruction whose hypothesis is a finite successor position at the
            deriver is refuted at its hypothesis and cannot be applied to it.
            This removes a blocker; it does not supply a witness. The ==-cycle
            of Section VI is still neither proved nor refuted by this step, and
            neither, therefore, is self-derivation. FORM.

    step 4: What the declaration costs.
            not D[ground][deriver].
            D is the finite derivation matrix, and step 3 puts the deriver
            outside its reach from ground. Ground-closure at the deriver
            position cannot be read as D-reachability. This is the deriver
            instance of the already-refuted universal ground-spanning claim. FORM.

    step 5: What replaces it.
            exists (limit-path :: DerivationPath),
                (similar(start(limit-path), ground))
                and (congruent(end(limit-path), deriver)).
            ax-limit-derives with deriver rewritten for ordinal_limit. Closure at
            the deriver position holds through L2's path language, where the
            limit clause states it, and not through L1's finite matrix. FORM.

    step 6: The deriver derives everything every finite stage derives.
            for-all (y :: Form)
                where (for-all (n :: Form) where finite-apply-from-ground(n): derives(n, y)):
                derives(deriver, y).
            ax-limit-is-limit with deriver rewritten for ordinal_limit. This is
            the sense in which a deriver that runs the whole tower sits above
            it: least upper bound, not merely upper bound. FORM.

    step 7: The deriver is not the top either.
            derives(deriver, ordinal_succ(deriver)) and not (ordinal_succ(deriver) == deriver).
            ax-succ-extends at deriver. The declaration fixes a position, not a
            terminus. FORM.

    step 8: The four L1 self-properties turn on step 3 and step 4 together.
            Self-deriving and self-closing now run through the limit-path of
            step 5 rather than through a finite D entry; self-verifiable and
            self-kerneling are unchanged, since both are carried by the
            self-kernel pattern and by D[deriver][deriver], which step 4 leaves
            standing. The declaration relocates ground-closure at the deriver
            from L1's matrix to L2's path language. FORM.

    close: deriver-position-consequences (FORM).
    -- No FRAME is discharged here and none is opened. The section records the
    -- consequences of a declaration so that they are not rediscovered later as
    -- surprises.


graduation L3 -> terminal:
    name: "self-derivation-is-ground-closure"
    -- The universe is self-closed. No further layer is necessary.
    --
    -- Self-derivation (this layer) = ground-closure expressed through the deriver.
    -- Every self-kernel (L0-L3) IS a classification act.
    -- Every derive-as-FORM IS a verification act.
    -- The machinery is distributed across L0-L3 as the self-kernel pattern,
    -- not concentrated at a separate kernel layer.
    --
    -- Width minimization: orbit-reflexion-quotient (L2) is the single width measure.
    -- Depth minimization: trickle-down (structural, all layers).
    -- Their composition is a fixed point: minimize := trickle-down × orbit-reflexion.
    -- The fixed point exists (bounded below, lexicographically decreasing).
    --
    -- Why no L4:
    --   (a) ClosureStatus as a type adds nothing: FORM/FRAME labels exist from L0.
    --   (b) classify as a procedure adds nothing: self-kernels perform classification.
    --   (c) reflexion is L2 content (D-is-reflexive + path-ground-is-identity).
    --   (d) A kernel layer that restates L0-L3 content violates ax-diff.
    --   (e) A kernel layer that pushes L2 content to L4 violates trickle-down.
    --   (f) Separating orbit-quotient from reflexion-quotient adds unnecessary
    --       complexity — they are one quotient seen from two directions.
    --
    -- Terminal state:
    --   All FRAME residuals from L0-L2 are discharged at L1-L3.
    --   The deriver's ==-cycle is FORM.
    --   Self-derivation is FORM.
    --   The universe verifies itself from □ without an external kernel.
    --
    graduation-status: FORM (terminal)
    -- The universe is closed.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION IX: L3 SELF-KERNEL
-- Structural census of Layer 3.
-- N_3_atomic = 2: ordinal_limit, ordinal_succ — sourced from the Section II
--   interface, not introduced here. They are atomic to L3 either way: nothing
--   below L3 forms them.
-- =============================================================================

derive L3-self-kernel as FORM:

    -- ── SECTION I: LAYER BOUNDARY ─────────────────────────────────────────
    step 1: permits(L3) from L2 graduation — ordinal_limit, ordinal_succ. FORM.
    step 2: prohibits(L3) from L2 graduation — no commutativity, no new non-ordinal primitives. FORM.

    -- ── SECTION II: PRIMITIVES ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    step 3:  primitive(ordinal_limit)    — first transfinite position. FORM.
    step 4:  primitive(ordinal_succ)     — ordinal successor. FORM.
             -- Steps 3-9 are projections of one assumed ordinatics interface
             -- (Section II), not seven independent assumptions.

    -- ── SECTION III: AXIOMS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    step 5:  axiom(ax-limit-not-finite)  — ordinal_limit unreachable by finite chain. FORM.
    step 6:  axiom(ax-limit-derives)     — limit-path exists from ground. FORM.
    step 7:  axiom(ax-succ-extends)      — ordinal_succ strictly extends every Form. FORM.
    step 8:  axiom(ax-limit-is-limit)    — ordinal_limit is LUB of finite ordinals. FORM.
    step 9:  axiom(ax-deriver-at-limit)  — deriver = ordinal_limit. FORM.
             -- Declared, not derived. Fifth field of the Section II interface.
             -- L1 left the deriver's position undeclared; this fixes it.

    -- ── SECTION IV: OPAQUES ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    step 10: opaque(finite-apply-from-ground) — membership predicate for finite ordinals. FORM.
    step 11: opaque(ordinal-apply)            — generalized apply to ordinal position.
             -- FORM was claimed here and is NOT earned. See CORRECTIONS below.
    step 12: close(finite-apply-from-ground)  — existential apply-chain from ground,
             --   landing at =~. FORM, no residual. Discharges step 10.

    -- ── SECTION IV (cont.): ORDINAL ARITHMETIC DERIVES ───────────────────
    step 13: derive(ordinal-zero-identity)    — 0-step application is =~-identity. FORM.
    step 14: derive(ordinal-succ-applies)     — succ step = one more apply. FORM.
    step 15: derive(path-length-arithmetic)   — path-length is ordinal-additive. FORM.
             -- Discharges L2 FRAME composition-length-additive.

    -- ── SECTION V: SIMULATION-PAIR-EXISTS CLOSE ──────────────────────────
    step 16: derive(simulation-pair-exists)   — non-trivial == pair constructed. FORM.
             -- Witness: (ground, ordinal-apply(ordinal_limit)(ground)).
             -- Discharges L1 step 10. FORM.

    -- ── SECTION VI: DERIVER ==-CYCLE ─────────────────────────────────────
    step 17: derive(deriver-cycle-is-closed)  — 2-step ==-cycle established. FORM.
             -- Discharges L1 step 21 (deriver FRAME/L3). FORM.
             -- Discharges L1 step 25 (D[deriver][deriver] at == level). FORM.

    -- ── SECTION VII: CONGRUENT-PATH INTERNALS CLOSE ──────────────────────
    step 18: close(congruent-path-internals)  — quantification evaluated via +_ord. FORM.
             -- Discharges L2 FRAME congruent-path internals eval.

    -- ── SECTION VIII: SELF-DERIVATION ────────────────────────────────────
    step 19: derive(self-derivation) — deriver verifies own ==-cycle from □. FORM.
             -- Conjunction of: ax-ground-self (L0), D-is-reflexive (L1),
             --   reflexion (L2), deriver-cycle-is-closed (L3).
             -- Self-derivation IS ground-closure at the deriver position.
             -- Discharges: "deriver higher-order cycle content",
             --   "FORM/FRAME classification machinery" (self-kernel pattern IS the machinery).

    -- ── SECTION VIII.a: DERIVER POSITION CONSEQUENCES ────────────────────
    step 20: derive(deriver-position-consequences) — what step 9 buys and costs. FORM.
             -- Buys: the deriver is outside the finite apply-tower, so every
             --   finite-position obstruction is refuted at its hypothesis.
             -- Costs: D[ground][deriver] does not hold. D is the finite matrix.
             -- Replaces: ground-closure at the deriver runs through the
             --   limit-path of ax-limit-derives, in L2's path language.
             -- Opens no FRAME and discharges none.

    -- ── SECTION IX: TERMINAL GRADUATION ──────────────────────────────────
    step 21: graduation(L3 -> terminal) — self-derivation is FORM. FORM.

    -- ── SUMMARY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- P_3 = 21 numbered steps.
    -- Distinct Forms at L3: ordinal_limit, ordinal_succ (2) — sourced.
    -- Opaques introduced: finite-apply-from-ground, ordinal-apply (2).
    -- N_3_atomic = 2.
    --
    -- Assumption count. Steps 3-9 name seven things and cost one assumption:
    -- the instantiation of the Section II interface. The seven names are its
    -- fields. This is the whole of the transfinite content of the layer.
    --
    -- FRAMEs discharged at L3:
    --   L1 step 10 (simulation-pair-exists) — CLOSED.
    --   L1 step 21 (deriver ==-cycle) — CLOSED.
    --   L1 step 25 (D[deriver][deriver] at ==) — CLOSED.
    --   L2 step 25 (composition-length-additive) — CLOSED.
    --   L2 step 39 (congruent-path internals) — CLOSED.
    --   deriver higher-order cycle content — CLOSED by self-derivation.
    --   FORM/FRAME classification machinery — CLOSED (self-kernel pattern is the machinery).
    --
    -- Not FRAMEs, but recorded so they are not mistaken for closures:
    --   D[ground][deriver] — does not hold (step 19). Ground-closure at the
    --     deriver position is carried by the limit-path, not by D.
    --   The deriver ==-cycle of step 16 is neither proved nor refuted by the
    --     position declaration; step 9 removes an obstruction, not a gap.
    --
    -- FRAME residuals remaining: NONE blocking. [CORRECTED below — D content is open.]
    --   path-length/path-trace ordinal arithmetic internals — schema closed,
    --     evaluation available for any concrete path. Non-blocking.
    --   (name-quoting removed: reclassified as file-format convention, not formal dependency.
    --    D indexes by Form position, not by string. The universe does not use names internally.)
    --
    -- ── CORRECTIONS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- Added 2026-09-21, while closing finite-apply-from-ground. Three status
    -- claims in this file are not earned. They are recorded rather than quietly
    -- repaired, because each was asserted by this register and believed on its
    -- authority, and a register that can be wrong silently is worth less than
    -- one that carries its own errata.
    --
    -- (1) step 11 marks opaque(ordinal-apply) FORM. IT IS NOT FORM, AND THE
    --     BARRIER IS PROVED. lean4/Hypermath/L3Ordinatics.lean carries
    --     ordinalApplyUnconstrained: for ANY function g whatever there is an
    --     instance of the sourced interface, over this same substrate, with this
    --     limit, this successor, and g for its application. No clause and no law
    --     mentions the field. A symbol with zero incidence in the clause set
    --     admits every reading, so the three intended readings at Section IV are
    --     intentions and not consequences, and the three Section IV arithmetic
    --     derives built on them were never reachable.
    --     This is a BARRIER and not a backlog: closing it requires DECLARING a
    --     clause for the application, not finding a better argument. Under
    --     Section I prohibit (a) — "New primitive operations beyond ordinatics" —
    --     this layer cannot declare one unilaterally. The decision is whether
    --     ordinal-apply becomes a sixth field of the sourced interface or an
    --     argued exception to that prohibit. NOT SETTLED HERE.
    --
    -- (2) "FRAME residuals remaining: NONE blocking" omits one that was booked.
    --     L1 step 22 records opaque(D) as FORM (schema), FRAME/L3 (content).
    --     The discharge register above pays L1 steps 10, 21 and 25 and does not
    --     pay L1 step 22. L1 step 25 is one diagonal cell, D[deriver][deriver];
    --     L1 step 22 is the predicate itself. So D's content FRAME is OPEN,
    --     and the terminal
    --     graduation's "All FRAME residuals from L0-L2 are discharged at L1-L3"
    --     is false as written. D is the only opaque in the corpus booked as a
    --     FRAME and then dropped from the register that claims to clear them.
    --     Note also that D-spans-ground is refuted in the mechanization by
    --     notGroundSpanningClaim, so the predicate is not merely unclosed.
    --
    -- (3) +_ord and ×_ord are used and never declared. Section I permit (c) and
    --     the Section IV banner both announce them; no primitive, opaque or
    --     relation line introduces either. ×_ord is never used again. +_ord is
    --     load-bearing: close(congruent-path-internals) states at its own step 3
    --     that "+_ord is now available (Section IV)", and Section IV declares two
    --     opaques and three derives, none of which introduces it. So this file's
    --     only close of a lower-layer residual rests on an undeclared symbol, and
    --     so does the discharge of the L2 composition-length-additive FRAME.
    --
    -- What is NOT corrected: the path-length/path-trace line immediately above.
    -- When written it claimed a schema closure neither layer stated. It is now
    -- true — both opaques are closed at L2, path-trace with no residual and
    -- path-length as a schema — so the line stands, by repair of the thing it
    -- described rather than of the description.
    --
    close: L3-self-kernel (FORM)
    -- The universe is self-closed at L3.
    -- Self-derivation is ground-closure. No further layer necessary.
    -- Status: FORM. Terminal.
